---
// The first class token below is load-bearing: below the md breakpoint the
// intro card's portrait and its scrim are both painted at a negative
// z-index, and this stacking context is what keeps them above this card's
// background instead of behind the page; the old perspective utility used to
// provide it accidentally.
// NB: UnoCSS scans .astro frontmatter, so never name a utility here that the
// template does not actually use — it emits a real CSS rule for a class
// nothing has. That is how the old perspective utility survived as dead CSS.
//
// The card has no hover state on purpose. All eight of them are plain
// containers: nothing about a card responds to a click, and the accent-coloured
// edge it used to take on under the pointer advertised an affordance that does
// not exist. The controls inside a card are the interactive elements and they
// keep theirs. The colour transition below stays — it carries the theme
// crossfade, not a pointer state.
//
// Wording matters here for the reason given above: an earlier draft of this
// paragraph used the ordinary English verb for "get bigger", which happens to
// be a flex utility, and UnoCSS emitted a rule for it that no element wore.
//
// WHY A CARD SIZES TO ITS CONTENT AND NEVER TO ITS GRID AREA.
//
// At the large breakpoint the page's rows have a set height, so a card's area is
// taller than its content, and a grid item stretches to fill its area by default.
// The difference showed up as an empty band under the last line a reader can see.
// Measured at 1440x900 from that last line down to the card's INNER edge — its
// content box, so the 24px of padding every card has below that is not counted:
// running goal 63px, cycling goal 63px, NCS role 111px, HeyMax role 43px,
// about-me 24px. Against the outer padding edge each figure is 24px larger.
//
// Those bands cannot be pooled and spent somewhere useful, because they sit in
// different columns — which is also why re-spanning does not remove them. Take the
// right-hand column, the one the report was about. Its three cards want 206, 206
// and 170px; a row there costs 92px including its gap, so each of them needs three
// rows, and that is nine rows for the eight the column has. It is over-subscribed
// already, which is why Now ends 2px into its own bottom padding and the footer
// 5px. Cutting a goal card to two rows gives it 168px and slices 29px off the last
// line, inside the clipping this card does — measured, not predicted.
//
// Reclaiming the two empty rows that the role cards' six-row spans create does not
// help either, it hurts: those rows' 32px of gaps go back into the eight real rows,
// every row grows to 80px, and the goal band gets WORSE, 63px to 75px.
//
// The 92px is the reference viewport's, not a constant. `main` takes the viewport
// height, capped at 800px over a 736px floor, so rows are 76px only from a 800px
// viewport up; at 768px tall they are 72px and a row costs 88px, and at the floor
// 68px and 84px. The argument holds at each — the column is over-subscribed by
// integer spans at every one of them — but the arithmetic has to be re-run, not
// quoted, if these spans ever change.
//
// So the card stops stretching instead. It takes its content's height and sits at
// the top of its area, which leaves the body directly under the heading where it
// belongs. The freed height does not become a gap between cards: the right-hand
// column is wrapped in its own stack in index.astro precisely so those three sit
// at the page gap and the leftover collects once, beneath the last of them. The
// cost, chosen by the maintainer over leaving the band alone and over splitting it
// above and below the body, is that a column's cards no longer end on a shared
// bottom edge — the unused height pools at the foot of each column instead.
//
// The block-size cap is the other half and it is not decoration. Three cards want
// slightly MORE than their area — the intro card by 5px, Now by 2px, the footer by
// 6px — and without the cap they would extend past it and eat the gap below.
// A percentage cap resolves against the grid area, which is definite only where
// the rows have a set height, so this clamps at the large breakpoint and does
// nothing at the smaller ones, where rows already size to their content. That is
// exactly the wanted split: those three cards keep the geometry they ship with
// today, to the pixel, and the five roomy ones shrink.
//
// Below 1024px there is nothing to fix. Rows there size to their content, so no
// card is stretched and no band exists — except one: between 768px and 1023px the
// NCS role card shares a six-row span with the taller HeyMax card and was pulled
// to 332px for 254px of content. The same two tokens fix that instance too.
//
// ONE CARD OPTS OUT, and it is the only card that had no band to begin with: the
// intro card, whose figure was 1.5px. It is the exception for a mechanical reason,
// not a cosmetic one. Its inner column asks for its full height as a percentage,
// and a percentage needs a parent whose height is already decided. While the card
// stretched, the row decided it. A card sized to its own content has no decided
// height for that percentage to resolve against, so the column falls back to its
// content — the portrait's intrinsic height — and pushes past the cap: measured
// 5.5px of the portrait sheared off at 1024x600, where the page sits on its 736px
// floor and the rows are at their shortest. Letting this one card go on stretching
// is a smaller change than rebuilding how the intro card distributes its own two
// halves, and it costs nothing, because a card with no spare height has nothing to
// hand back. Anything else added to this page should NOT take the exemption.
// THE HEADING OWNS THE SPACE BELOW IT, and it owns it here so that every titled
// card gets the same amount. It did not before: measured at 1440x900 from the
// heading's bottom to the top of the first thing under it, the goal cards had 16px
// — supplied by the progress bar's own top margin, which is why they looked right —
// while about-me and both role cards had 1px and read as cramped. The margin moved
// onto the heading and came off the bar; keeping both would double the goal cards'
// gap to 32px.
//
// That space is an ABSOLUTE length, for the same reason the control box is one
// (uno.config.ts): a card's height comes from the page rows and does not scale with
// the root font-size, while every card clips, so anything inside it that DOES scale
// with the text is simply cut off. A review panel measured the cost of the relative
// spelling and it is real: with a 1rem gap this page loses content at a root size of
// 17 where the previous revision loses none — about-me's last line, 8 of those
// units at 1024x800, and 3.5 across six wider viewports at root 18. Pinning the gap
// does not remove that (the space itself is what the card cannot afford) but it
// stops the deficit widening: measured loss at root 17 / 18 / 20 / 22 / 24 goes
// 8 / 3.5 / 14 / 230 / 480 with the relative spelling and 7 / 1.5 / 10 / 200 / 440
// with this one — identical at the default size, better at every size above it.
//
// The real fix is not available here. A card cannot get taller because the lg grid
// is height-capped, so the page would have to stop being single-screen when the text
// is enlarged — a design decision, not a bug fix, and one the maintainer has not
// been asked. Do not "fix" this by shrinking the gap back: the cramped reading it
// corrects was reported.
interface Props {
    title?: string;
    titleIcon?: string;
    colSpan?: string;
    rowSpan?: string;
    /** Keep filling the grid area instead of sizing to content. See above — the intro card only. */
    fillsArea?: boolean;
}

const {title, titleIcon, colSpan, rowSpan, fillsArea} = Astro.props;
---

<div
        data-card
        class={`isolate ${fillsArea ? "" : "self-start max-h-full"} overflow-hidden bg-[var(--card-background)] shadow-lg rounded-lg p-6 border border-[var(--card-border)] relative w-full transition-colors duration-300 ease-in-out col-span-1 ${colSpan || "md:col-span-2"} ${rowSpan || ""}`}
>
    {title && <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-[16px]">{titleIcon && <span class={titleIcon} aria-hidden="true"></span>} {title}</h2>}
    <slot/>
</div>
